INTENT
Link creation on the existing URL shortener must be throttled per API key for callers that present a known key in an X-API-Key header, using per-key quotas configured entirely through the SHORTENER_API_KEYS environment variable, while callers with no key or an unrecognised key continue to be throttled by the current per-IP limiter with no observable change.

DESIGN
{
  "configuration": [
    {
      "default": "./links.db",
      "name": "LINKS_DB_PATH",
      "purpose": "Filesystem path to the SQLite database file (WAL mode). This is the single canonical name; the service must not read DATABASE_PATH. Tests set LINKS_DB_PATH to a tmp path and assert persistence against it."
    },
    {
      "default": "http://localhost:8000",
      "name": "LINKS_BASE_URL",
      "purpose": "Origin used to build the absolute short_url field in the POST /api/links response. Trailing slashes are stripped before concatenation."
    },
    {
      "default": "true",
      "name": "LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED",
      "purpose": "Master switch for all limiting. When false the middleware short-circuits: no per-IP bucket, no per-key bucket, no 429 is ever produced, and SHORTENER_API_KEYS has no effect."
    },
    {
      "default": "10",
      "name": "LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX",
      "purpose": "Maximum POST /api/links requests per window for the per-IP bucket. Applies only to callers without a recognised API key; a recognised key uses its own quota instead of, not in addition to, this value."
    },
    {
      "default": "60",
      "name": "LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS",
      "purpose": "Fixed-window length in seconds shared by the per-IP bucket and every per-key bucket. Also the upper clamp for the Retry-After value."
    },
    {
      "default": "false",
      "name": "LINKS_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR",
      "purpose": "When true the per-IP bucket identity is the leftmost entry of X-Forwarded-For, otherwise the transport peer address. Has no influence on keyed callers, whose counter is shared across all client addresses."
    },
    {
      "default": "\"\" (empty string)",
      "name": "SHORTENER_API_KEYS",
      "purpose": "Comma-separated key:quota pairs, e.g. \"alpha:100,beta:20\", giving per-minute POST /api/links quotas. Read once at startup only. Exact name, no LINKS_ prefix. Unset or empty means no key is ever recognised and behaviour is identical to before this change."
    }
  ],
  "data_model": [
    {
      "fields": [
        {
          "constraints": "PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, internal only, never exposed in any response",
          "name": "id",
          "type": "INTEGER"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, UNIQUE \u2014 this constraint is the sole enforcement point for short code uniqueness",
          "name": "code",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, <= 2048 chars, validated http/https before insert",
          "name": "target_url",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, RFC 3339 UTC",
          "name": "created_at",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NULL means never expires; RFC 3339 UTC otherwise",
          "name": "expires_at",
          "type": "TEXT"
        }
      ],
      "indexes": [
        "Implicit UNIQUE index on links(code): serves the redirect hot path SELECT target_url, expires_at FROM links WHERE code = ?, which is a single index lookup and never a table scan.",
        "No index on target_url: no query filters by it, and adding one would be a schema change."
      ],
      "name": "links",
      "purpose": "Existing table, unchanged by this work. One row per short link. PRAGMA table_info(links) must return exactly these columns after the change."
    },
    {
      "fields": [
        {
          "constraints": "PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT",
          "name": "id",
          "type": "INTEGER"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, references links(code) logically; no API key material is ever written here or anywhere else in SQLite",
          "name": "code",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, RFC 3339 UTC",
          "name": "clicked_at",
          "type": "TEXT"
        }
      ],
      "indexes": [
        "Existing index idx_clicks_code on clicks(code): required by the stats read path SELECT COUNT(*), MAX(clicked_at) FROM clicks WHERE code = ?, which would otherwise scan the whole clicks table as it grows. No new index is added by this change."
      ],
      "name": "clicks",
      "purpose": "Existing table, unchanged. One row per served redirect. PRAGMA table_info(clicks) must return exactly these columns after the change."
    },
    {
      "fields": [
        {
          "constraints": "MAP KEY. blake2b(presented_key_utf8, key=BOOT_SALT, digest_size=32) where BOOT_SALT is 32 bytes from secrets.token_bytes() generated once per process. The plaintext key is never retained as a dict key, attribute, log field or exception argument.",
          "name": "digest",
          "type": "bytes (32)"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, >= 1. Entries parsing to 0 or negative or non-integer are dropped at startup.",
          "name": "quota",
          "type": "int"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "Process-global, regenerated every boot, never logged or serialised.",
          "name": "BOOT_SALT",
          "type": "bytes (32)"
        }
      ],
      "indexes": [
        "Dict keyed by digest: recognition is a single constant-work hash plus a hash-table lookup, so no early-exit comparison loop over configured keys exists to time. hmac.compare_digest is used on the retrieved digest as belt and braces."
      ],
      "name": "ApiKeyQuotaTable (in-process, immutable after startup)",
      "purpose": "Parsed form of SHORTENER_API_KEYS. Built once during application startup, held on app.state, never mutated at runtime, never persisted."
    },
    {
      "fields": [
        {
          "constraints": "Existing structure, unchanged. Key is the per-boot salted hash of the client address. Holds unkeyed, blank-key and unknown-key callers. Pruned to MAX_TRACKED_KEYS by the existing policy.",
          "name": "ip_buckets",
          "type": "dict[bytes, (window_start_float, count_int)]"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "New. Key is the same per-boot salted digest used by ApiKeyQuotaTable, so no plaintext key sits in memory. An entry is created only for a digest already present in ApiKeyQuotaTable, so unknown keys allocate nothing and cannot grow this map; it is additionally bounded by MAX_TRACKED_KEYS with the existing pruning.",
          "name": "key_buckets",
          "type": "dict[bytes, (window_start_float, count_int)]"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "Defaults to time.monotonic. Public attribute on the limiter instance (app.state.rate_limiter.clock) so tests advance the window by substituting a fake clock rather than sleeping.",
          "name": "clock",
          "type": "callable() -> float"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "Existing lock; every read-modify-write of either bucket map happens inside it, because create_link is a sync endpoint run in Starlette's threadpool.",
          "name": "lock",
          "type": "threading.Lock"
        }
      ],
      "indexes": [
        "Both maps are O(1) dict lookups on the hot path; no scan.",
        "MAX_TRACKED_KEYS remains a module-level constant (10000) applied to each map independently, not an environment variable."
      ],
      "name": "RateLimiterState (in-process, mutable, guarded by threading.Lock)",
      "purpose": "Existing fixed-window counters extended with a second namespace for keyed callers. Lost on restart, as required."
    }
  ],
  "decisions": [
    {
      "choice": "Exclusive substitution: if X-API-Key exactly matches a configured key, only the per-key bucket is incremented and only its quota is checked; the per-IP bucket is neither read nor written for that request.",
      "decision": "How a recognised key interacts with the existing per-IP bucket",
      "rationale": "A caller holding alpha:100 behind LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10 gets 100 creations, and the 11th keyed creation from one address is a 201 rather than a surprising 429 \u2014 the quota means what the operator configured.",
      "rejected": [
        "Enforce both buckets (min of the two): the configured 100 would silently become 10 and the acceptance case of 11 keyed creations would fail.",
        "Add the key quota on top of the IP allowance: makes the effective limit depend on IP, so the same key gets different totals from different hosts."
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Both fall through to the unchanged per-IP bucket and are indistinguishable in every response. A header absent, present but empty, or whitespace-only is treated as absent; a non-empty value that is not byte-equal to a configured key (including ALPHA vs alpha) is unknown. Unknown keys allocate no per-key bucket.",
      "decision": "What counts as 'no key' versus 'unknown key'",
      "rationale": "A typo or a stale key degrades to today's behaviour instead of producing a distinct error, and mixed unknown-keyed and unkeyed traffic from one address shares one counter (10 succeed, the 11th is 429) so an attacker cannot buy extra allowance by inventing header values.",
      "rejected": [
        "401/403 for unrecognised keys: changes observable behaviour for existing callers and turns the header into a key-existence oracle.",
        "Bucketing unknown keys separately: unbounded memory growth from random header values, and a free per-value quota."
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "GET /{code} returns 404 with the identical body {\"error\":{\"code\":\"not_found\",\"message\":\"No such link.\"}} and identical headers for both cases. Only GET /api/links/{code}/stats reveals expiry, via 200 with expired=true.",
      "decision": "What a request for an expired code returns and whether it is distinguishable from a code that never existed",
      "rationale": "A visitor sees the same dead-link page either way, and nobody can use the redirect endpoint to confirm that a particular code was once live and has lapsed. The owner who knows the code can still read the final click count.",
      "rejected": [
        "410 Gone for expired: a free enumeration oracle that separates 'was real' from 'never existed' across the whole code space.",
        "Deleting expired rows on read: destroys the stats the owner is entitled to and makes the redirect path a write path."
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Synchronously, inside the redirect handler, committed before the 307 is returned; the insert into clicks and the lookup share the same connection and transaction.",
      "decision": "Where the click analytics write happens relative to the redirect response",
      "rationale": "A click that produced a redirect is always counted, so stats never lag or silently lose events under load, and tests can assert click_count immediately after a redirect without polling. The added cost is one indexed insert on a WAL-mode local file.",
      "rejected": [
        "Starlette BackgroundTask after the response: counts become racy \u2014 a test or dashboard reading straight after a redirect sees a stale number, and events are lost if the worker dies.",
        "In-memory batch flushed periodically: loses counts on restart, and the limiter is already the only tolerable in-memory-loss component.",
        "Skipping counting entirely on redirect: defeats the reason 301 was rejected."
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "A pure ASGI middleware that runs before routing and before body parsing, scoped to exactly method == POST and path == /api/links. Identity is the per-boot salted digest of the API key when the key is recognised, otherwise the per-boot salted digest of the client address (leftmost X-Forwarded-For when LINKS_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=true, else the peer address).",
      "decision": "At which layer rate limiting is enforced and what it is keyed by",
      "rationale": "A throttled request never opens a SQLite connection and never writes a row, so a flood costs one dict lookup; /health, /{code} and the stats endpoint can never be refused with 429 becaus

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
- With SHORTENER_API_KEYS="alpha:100,beta:20", LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true and LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10, 100 consecutive POST /api/links requests carrying header X-API-Key: alpha within one window all return 201, and the 101st returns 429.
- The 429 produced by exceeding a key quota has body {"error":{"code":"rate_limited","message":...}}, a Retry-After header whose value parses as an integer >= 1 and <= 60, Cache-Control: no-store, and leaves the links table row count unchanged (no row written for the refused request).
- With SHORTENER_API_KEYS="alpha:100,beta:20", 20 creations with X-API-Key: beta return 201 and the 21st returns 429, proving the quota is read per key and not shared.
- After key alpha has been throttled to 429, the next POST /api/links carrying X-API-Key: beta returns 201, proving quotas are independent per key.
- With LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10 and quota alpha:100, 11 consecutive keyed creations from one client address all return 201, proving a known key is limited by its quota only and not additionally by the per-IP bucket.
- With LINKS_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=true and quota alpha:3, three creations sent from X-Forwarded-For 203.0.113.10 with key alpha followed by one creation from X-Forwarded-For 198.51.100.7 with key alpha returns 429 for the fourth, proving one key's counter is shared across client addresses.
- With no X-API-Key header, LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10 and LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true, the 11th creation from one address returns 429 with Retry-After, exactly as the current suite already asserts.
- A request with X-API-Key: gamma (not present in SHORTENER_API_KEYS) is counted in the same per-IP bucket as a request with no key at all: 10 mixed keyed-unknown and unkeyed creations from one address succeed and the 11th returns 429.
- Key matching is exact and case-sensitive: with alpha configured, X-API-Key: ALPHA is treated as unknown and falls back to the per-IP limit.
- An X-API-Key header present but empty, or whitespace only, is treated as no key and falls back to the per-IP limit.
- With SHORTENER_API_KEYS unset or set to an empty string, every request behaves exactly as today; the X-API-Key header has no effect on any response.
- Malformed entries in SHORTENER_API_KEYS ("alpha" with no colon, "beta:abc", "gamma:0", "gamma:-5", ":5", empty segments) are ignored at startup while any well formed entry in the same string still takes effect; the service starts and serves requests normally.
- SHORTENER_API_KEYS=" alpha : 100 , beta:20 " yields working quotas of 100 for alpha and 20 for beta (surrounding whitespace around keys, quotas and separators is stripped).
- When the same key appears twice, e.g. "alpha:5,alpha:9", the last occurrence wins and the effective quota is 9.
- After the 60 second window has elapsed (simulated by advancing the limiter's clock, not by sleeping), a previously throttled key's next creation returns 201.
- GET /{code} redirects return 307 regardless of the X-API-Key header and regardless of whether that key's creation quota is exhausted; a keyed caller whose quota is spent can still follow links.
- GET /api/links/{code}/stats and GET /health are never refused with 429 on account of any API key quota.
- With LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false, 15 consecutive creations carrying X-API-Key: beta (quota 20) and 15 carrying no key all return 201; no limiting of any kind occurs.
- No schema change: after exercising keyed and unkeyed creation, PRAGMA table_info(links) and PRAGMA table_info(clicks) return exactly the columns they return today and no additional tables exist.
- After driving a key past its quota, the raw key string does not appear in any file under the database directory (including the WAL) and does not appear in any emitted log record.
- No new HTTP route is added: the OpenAPI paths after the change are exactly /health, /api/links, /api/links/{code}/stats (plus the unlisted /{code}).
- Every existing test in tests/ passes unchanged after the change.

CONSTRAINTS
- Python 3 with FastAPI 0.115.6, Starlette 0.41.3, Pydantic 2.10.4; no new runtime dependency may be added to requirements.txt.
- No SQLite schema change: no new table, column or index; limiter state stays in process memory and is lost on restart.
- No admin surface: no new route, no runtime mutation of the key set; keys and quotas are read from the process environment only.
- The environment variable is named exactly SHORTENER_API_KEYS (no LINKS_ prefix), holding comma-separated key:quota pairs.
- The header is exactly X-API-Key (HTTP header matching is case-insensitive on the name).
- The quota unit is requests per minute for POST /api/links only.
- Refusal must use the existing error envelope {"error":{"code":"rate_limited","message":...}} with HTTP 429 and a Retry-After header containing whole seconds as a decimal integer, minimum 1.
- Existing behaviour for unkeyed and unknown-key callers must be byte-for-byte unchanged: same status codes, same buckets, same env var names and defaults (LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX, LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS, LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED, LINKS_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR).
- Limiting must continue to run as ASGI middleware before routing and body parsing, so a throttled request never opens a database connection.
- API keys are secrets: they must never be written to SQLite, to a log record, to an exception message or to a response body, and must not be included in the error message returned on 429.
- Key lookup must not leak the configured key material through a timing side channel that is cheaper than brute forcing the key itself: compare with hmac.compare_digest or look up a salted hash of the presented key rather than doing early-exit string comparison over the configured set.
- Limiter state must be keyed by a per-boot salted hash of the presented key, matching the existing treatment of client addresses, so no key material sits in memory structures that could be dumped as plaintext keys.
- The tracked-key map must stay bounded (the existing MAX_TRACKED_KEYS pruning must continue to apply) so an attacker sending random X-API-Key values cannot grow memory without limit; unknown keys must not allocate a per-key bucket at all.
- Counting must remain thread safe under the existing threading.Lock, because create_link is a sync endpoint executed in Starlette's threadpool.
- README.md must document SHORTENER_API_KEYS in the configuration table and correct the current claim that no secrets are read by the service.

Implement this design as a complete, runnable service. Include requirements.txt with pinned versions. The next stage generates tests against the files you return and reviews them line by line, so return the code you would defend in that review.

