import logging

# The columns the implementation actually creates today.
LINKS_COLUMNS = ["id", "code", "url", "created_at", "expires_at"]
CLICKS_COLUMNS = ["id", "link_id", "clicked_at", "referrer", "user_agent"]


def test_keyed_and_unkeyed_traffic_leaves_the_table_columns_unchanged(app_factory):
    app = app_factory(SHORTENER_API_KEYS="alpha:5", LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="true")
    code = app.create("https://example.com/a", key="alpha").json()["code"]
    app.create("https://example.com/b")
    assert app.visit(code).status_code == 307

    assert app.columns("links") == LINKS_COLUMNS, app.columns("links")
    assert app.columns("clicks") == CLICKS_COLUMNS, app.columns("clicks")


def test_no_table_beyond_links_and_clicks_is_created_for_api_keys(app_factory):
    app = app_factory(SHORTENER_API_KEYS="alpha:5", LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="true")
    app.create("https://example.com/a", key="alpha")

    assert app.table_names() == ["clicks", "links"], app.table_names()


def test_the_openapi_document_lists_exactly_the_three_public_paths(app):
    body = app.client.get("/openapi.json").json()

    assert set(body["paths"]) == {
        "/health",
        "/api/links",
        "/api/links/{code}/stats",
    }, sorted(body["paths"])


def test_the_unlisted_redirect_route_still_answers(app):
    code = app.create("https://example.com/a").json()["code"]

    assert app.visit(code).status_code == 307


def test_the_api_key_never_reaches_the_database_directory_or_the_logs(app_factory, caplog):
    caplog.set_level(logging.DEBUG)
    secret = "s3cr3t-DO-NOT-PERSIST"
    app = app_factory(
        SHORTENER_API_KEYS="{0}:2".format(secret),
        LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="true",
        LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX="50",
    )

    assert app.create("https://example.com/1", key=secret).status_code == 201
    code = app.create("https://example.com/2", key=secret).json()["code"]
    refused = app.create("https://example.com/3", key=secret)
    assert refused.status_code == 429, refused.text[:200]
    assert app.visit(code, key=secret).status_code == 307

    assert secret.encode() not in app.db_dir_bytes(), (
        "the raw API key was written to the database directory (including WAL)"
    )
    assert secret not in caplog.text, "the raw API key was written to a log record"
    assert secret not in refused.text, "the 429 body echoed the API key back"
"}],"coverage_notes":"Location: all new tests live in tests_pipeline/ (a package, so its conftest and module names cannot collide with the existing tests/ directory). Fresh SQLite file + freshly imported application module per test (startup-only SHORTENER_API_KEYS is re-parsed, limiter state starts empty); a stub resolver replaces socket.getaddrinfo so nothing touches the network; time is never slept, only advanced through a substituted limiter clock.

Mandated coverage: round trip -> test_link_round_trip_contract.py (201 body, exact 307, exact Location, Cache-Control: no-store, LINKS_BASE_URL trailing-slash stripping). Open redirect -> test_open_redirect_scheme_rejection.py (javascript:/data:/file: get 4xx with error envelope, unsupported_scheme, and a separate test proving zero rows exist, plus a CRLF-injection URL). Private/link-local -> test_private_network_target_rejection.py (169.254.169.254, 127.0.0.1, 10/172.16/192.168, ::1, fd00::, 0.0.0.0, hostnames resolving to metadata/loopback/mixed, plus a public positive control so 'deny everything' fails). Expiry -> test_link_expiry_behaviour.py (expiry rewritten into the past in the DB; no sleeps). Rate limit -> test_per_ip_rate_limit.py (11th unkeyed request 429 + Retry-After + no row; a second address still 201; LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX honoured; window advance via fake clock; redirects/stats/health never throttled; LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false). Collision -> test_short_code_collision_retry.py (generator forced to return an existing code once/twice/three times; asserts retry happened, unique code, both links still resolve, never 500; the stub falls back to random codes after the queue drains so an infinite-retry implementation cannot hang the suite). Unknown code -> test_unknown_code_handling.py (404 not_found, zero links, zero clicks, malformed codes). Click analytics -> test_click_analytics_counting.py (+1 per 307, no increment on 404 or on a refused expired redirect, per-link attribution, count readable synchronously).

Acceptance criteria for SHORTENER_API_KEYS -> test_api_key_quota_limits.py: 100 alpha creations then 429; 429 envelope/Retry-After 1..60/Cache-Control no-store/no row; beta quota 20 with 21st 429; independence of alpha and beta counters; 11 keyed creations under LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10 (exclusive substitution); one key's counter shared across X-Forwarded-For addresses; unknown key shares the per-IP bucket; ALPHA vs alpha case sensitivity; empty/whitespace header treated as absent; SHORTENER_API_KEYS unset behaves as today; malformed entries dropped while epsilon:12 works; whitespace stripping in ' alpha : 100 , beta:20 '; duplicate key last-wins (9 not 5); window advance restores a throttled key; redirects and stats/health unaffected by a spent quota; limiter disabled -> 15 keyed + 15 unkeyed all 201. test_surface_invariants.py covers no schema change, no extra table, OpenAPI paths exactly {/health, /api/links, /api/links/{code}/stats} plus the unlisted /{code}, and that the raw key appears in no file under the DB directory (incl. WAL), no log record and no response body.

Design vs implementation mismatches (findings, tests follow the implementation as instructed):
1. Expired links: the design says GET /{code} must return 404 not_found, indistinguishable from an unknown code, to avoid an enumeration oracle. The implementation returns 410 with error code link_expired. Tests assert 410/link_expired. This is a real deviation from the stated security decision.
2. Data model names: the design specifies links.target_url and clicks(code, clicked_at); the implementation uses links.url and clicks(id, link_id, clicked_at, referrer, user_agent). The 'no schema change' test pins the implementation's columns.
3. Limiter internals: the design pins app.state.rate_limiter.clock as the substitution point and a fixed-window counter with per-IP and per-key maps; the implementation uses a sliding-window deque inside the middleware with no exposed clock and no key awareness at all (app/ratelimit.py never reads X-API-Key and app/config.py never reads SHORTENER_API_KEYS). The clock fixture prefers .clock and falls back to shimming the limiter module's time module; the window tests assert a clock was actually installed. Every keyed-quota test will fail against the code as shown, which is the point: the feature is unimplemented there.
4. The implementation reads several env vars not in the design's configuration list (LINKS_DEFAULT_TTL_DAYS, LINKS_MAX_URL_LENGTH, LINKS_CODE_LENGTH, LINKS_CODE_MAX_ATTEMPTS, LINKS_STATS_*, LINKS_DNS_RESOLUTION_ENABLED, LINKS_LOG_LEVEL). These tests set none of them; DNS is neutralised at the socket layer instead, so behaviour is pinned only against design-sanctioned names.

Deliberately not covered here: default TTL length, max URL length, stats pagination bounds, health degradation, log redaction of client IPs, code entropy/statistics, and the design's stats 'expired=true' field (the implementation exposes no such field) - the pre-existing tests/ suite covers most of these."}

